### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1. **Structural**: The central claim—that the paper introduces novel explainability methods that “close the explainability gap” and build trust for experts and citizen intelligence—is falsified by the paper’s own results, and rests on methods that are not novel but appropriated from vision and NLP.
   - The abstract claims the paper “introduce novel explainability methods” whose results “build trust with human experts” and unlock “citizen intelligence.” Section 4, however, states explicitly, “We appropriate methods for vision and natural language explainability and translate them to the audio domain.” Occlusion (Section 4.1) and attention roll-out (Section 4.2) are standard techniques (Zeiler & Fergus 2014; Abnar & Zuidema 2020) applied to spectrograms or wav2vec tokens, not novel audio-specific methods.
   - Section 5.2 shows occlusion fails outright: importance concentrates on padded regions that “theoretically contain no predictive information,” a result the authors themselves call “obviously unhelpful.” Attention roll-out is never validated with human experts or lay users; Section 7 concedes the methods “may not yet offer insights that are as intuitive to non-technical users,” directly contradicting the citizen-intelligence motivation. No human evaluation of any kind is conducted.
   - Because the title, abstract, and introduction predicate the contribution on closing the explainability gap and enabling trustworthy deployment, and the body demonstrates the opposite, the paper’s framing is invalid.

2. **Structural**: The “generalizability benchmark” is merely a single cross-dataset experiment, not a benchmark, and its confounded design cannot support robust conclusions about real-world generalization.
   - The abstract claims the authors “open-source a novel benchmark for real-world generalizability.” Section 6, however, describes only one experiment: training three models on ASVspoof 5 and evaluating on 3,000 balanced samples from FakeAVCeleb (Table 1). No standardized protocol, release procedure, or leaderboard is described in the body.
   - The experiment conflates generalization to unseen deepfake algorithms with generalization to new acoustic domains (likely differing languages, recording equipment, compression, and speaker demographics). The GBDT’s collapse to ~51% accuracy likely reflects the failure of hand-crafted features to transfer across radical acoustic conditions rather than a meaningful measurement of deepfake-detection robustness. Labeling this one-off train/test split a “novel benchmark” mischaracterizes the work and overstates its utility to the community.

3. **Structural**: The “conceptual explainability framework” promised as the first contribution does not exist.
   - The introduction lists as a contribution: “A conceptual explainability framework for deepfake audio detection (Section 4).” Section 3.3 provides only a brief definitions paragraph distinguishing interpretability from explainability and three informal desiderata (sample-specific, time-specific, feature-specific). Section 4 contains exclusively procedural descriptions of occlusion and attention roll-out. There is no framework—no taxonomy, no formal criteria, no comparative rubric, no synthesis—organizing or evaluating explainability methods for audio deepfake detection. This contribution is vacuous.

## Section-by-Section Notes

- **Abstract and Introduction**: The abstract and introduction repeatedly claim that the paper “introduce novel explainability methods” and that attention roll-out “addresses these shortcomings by providing improved explainability.” These claims are incompatible with Section 4’s admission that the methods are appropriated from vision/NLP and with Section 5.2’s demonstration that occlusion is “unhelpful.” The citizen-intelligence and trust-building motivation cited in the introduction (Goodman, 2024; Staff, 2024) has no corresponding human-subjects experiment in the body.
- **Section 3.3**: The three desiderata (sample-specific, time-specific, feature-specific) are sensible but never used to systematically evaluate the methods. For example, Section 4.1 asserts that occlusion “delivers on all three aspects of sufficient explainability defined in 3.3” without justification; in practice, spectrogram-patch occlusion yields time/frequency localization, not feature-specific acoustic explanations like “errant formants.”
- **Section 4.2 / Equation (11)**: The attention roll-out formulation ignores residual connections. The paper defines standard transformer blocks with residual links in Equation (7), yet computes roll-out by simply multiplying raw attention matrices $W^{(1)} \dots W^{(L)}$. Standard attention roll-out (Abnar & Zuidema, 2020) requires incorporating identity matrices to account for residual pathways. This misapplication may distort the computed token importance.
- **Section 5.1**: The GBDT feature-importance analysis (Figure 2) finds RMS suspiciously dominant, but the subsequent ablations are peripheral to the paper’s central claims about transformers and explainability. They read as self-contained traditional-method experiments without clear integration into the broader argument.
- **Section 5.2**: The authors deserve credit for honestly reporting occlusion’s failure. However, they do not diagnose why zero-padding dominates (e.g., patch-embedding artifacts, [CLS] token bias) or test alternative occlusion baselines (mean, noise), weakening the experimental completeness.
- **Table 1 / Section 6**: Wav2Vec exhibits extreme precision/recall imbalance on FakeAVCeleb (97% precision spoof vs. 63% recall spoof; 73% precision bonafide vs. 98% recall bonafide), suggesting it may be exploiting dataset-specific acoustic biases rather than generalizing robustly to deepfake artifacts. The paper does not analyze this asymmetry, yet draws conclusions about AST’s superior balanced performance without probing the cause.
- **Section 7**: The acknowledged limitations (only two datasets, methods “in their infancy”) do not reconcile with the magnitude of the abstract’s claims. The paper cannot simultaneously concede that explanations are not intuitive to non-technical users and claim to pave the way for citizen intelligence.

## Strengths

1. The paper reports the negative result that occlusion importance collapses onto padded regions (Section 5.2). Such honesty about method failure is valuable and under-reported in the XAI literature.
2. The cross-dataset comparison in Section 6 reveals a potentially useful empirical difference between AST and Wav2Vec: AST achieves far more balanced precision/recall on FakeAVCeleb (Table 1), suggesting architecture choice matters for out-of-distribution robustness.
3. The three desiderata for audio deepfake explainability introduced in Section 3.3 (sample-specific, time-specific, feature-specific) are conceptually sensible and could serve as a useful starting point if developed into an actual framework.

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Human-subjects validation of explanations.** The paper makes strong claims about trust and citizen intelligence but includes no study with experts or laypeople evaluating whether attention roll-out or occlusion outputs are understandable, trustworthy, or actionable. Add a user study measuring explanation usefulness or human-AI team detection accuracy.
2. **Controlled domain-shift ablations.** To justify calling the evaluation a “benchmark,” add experiments that disentangle acoustic domain shift from deepfake-algorithm shift—e.g., same-dataset cross-attack evaluation or condition-matched sub-splits—to prove the protocol measures generalization rather than dataset bias.
3. **Occlusion with appropriate baselines.** The current failure mode (zero-padding dominance) may be an artifact of the occlusion value. Experiment with mean-value, noise, or interpolated occlusion to determine whether the method is fundamentally broken on spectrograms or merely badly instantiated.

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Root-cause analysis of Wav2Vec’s precision/recall imbalance on FakeAVCeleb (Table 1).** Does the model exploit non-speech characteristics (background noise, microphone frequency response) to separate datasets rather than deepfake artifacts? This is critical for any claim about real-world robustness.
2. **Formal mapping between the Section 3.3 desiderata and the evaluated methods.** The paper should systematically assess whether attention roll-out and occlusion actually satisfy sample-, time-, and feature-specificity, rather than asserting without argument that they do.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Acoustic grounding of attention roll-out.** Show that high-attention tokens correspond to audible artifacts (e.g., formant discontinuities, metallic noise) via zoomed spectrograms and audio snippets, confirming that attention aligns with known deepfake acoustic signatures rather than spurious correlations.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Evaluation of gradient-based attributions (e.g., Integrated Gradients, LRP).** Given that occlusion fails due to padding artifacts, gradient methods that do not require masking may provide more stable audio explanations and should have been included as a comparison.

## Overall Assessment

The paper’s central narrative—that it closes the explainability gap in audio deepfake detection via novel methods and a new benchmark—is structurally unsound. The methods are not novel but borrowed from vision and NLP; the occlusion results are openly admitted to be “unhelpful”; attention roll-out lacks any human validation; the “benchmark” is a single confounded cross-dataset split; and the promised conceptual framework is absent. The abstract’s claims about trust, citizen intelligence, and open-sourcing are entirely unsupported by the body. While the paper contains one genuinely interesting negative result (occlusion failure) and an intriguing empirical difference between AST and Wav2Vec generalization, these are insufficient to rescue a submission whose core contributions are overstated to the point of invalidity. The paper should not be accepted in its current form unless it is fundamentally rewritten as an empirical analysis of why standard XAI methods fail on audio transformers, with correspondingly restrained claims.
### Strength Finder
 ## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **Attention rollout is successfully adapted to audio transformers, yielding time-localized explanations.** Section 4.2 explicitly states that attention rollout was previously used only for natural language tokens and adapts the method to audio tokens via the cumulative attention matrix in Equation 11. Figure 5 shows the resulting normalized [CLS] token attention distribution across Token IDs, revealing grouped, influential 20 ms frames. This directly delivers sample- and time-specific explanations for transformer-based detectors, addressing a gap the authors identify in Section 2: “The literature for audio explainability is limited... very few examples of attempts for audio explainability exist in the literature.”

2. **A novel cross-dataset benchmark provides rigorous evidence of real-world generalization gaps and unexpected robustness rankings.** Section 6 introduces a protocol training on ASVspoof 5 and testing on FakeAVCeleb, and Table 1 reports that the AST achieves a balanced F1 of 0.85 on out-of-distribution data, while the widely favored Wav2Vec model exhibits severe class skew (spoof F1 0.77) and the GBDT collapses to near-random performance (~0.51 accuracy). This quantifies the real-world robustness problem the paper targets and provides the surprising empirical finding that AST generalizes better than the more popular Wav2Vec alternative.

3. **Empirical diagnoses expose concrete failure modes of standard explainability methods.** Section 5.2 applies occlusion (Section 4.1) to AST spectrograms and finds that importance concentrates spuriously in padded regions (Figure 4), diagnosing a non-semantic position bias. Separately, Section 5.1 uses permutation importance (Algorithm 1/Section 3.3) to show the GBDT relies disproportionately on RMS (loudness) (Figure 2), which the authors note is “troubling” as a non-inherent deepfake signature. These critical findings go beyond method application to reveal domain-specific inadequacies that future work must address.

### Supporting strengths

1. **Multicollinearity-aware feature importance analysis for the GBDT baseline.** Section 3.3 describes hierarchical clustering of Spearman rank-order correlations to handle multicollinearity, Figure 3 visualizes the resulting clusters, and Section 5.1 validates the approach by showing that retraining on one feature per cluster degrades accuracy to 63.8% versus 70% when using the top three correlated features. This methodological rigor distinguishes the analysis from prior GBDT work (e.g., Bird & Lotfi, 2023) that reported feature importances without addressing redundancy among audio signal features.

2. **Conceptual framework defines domain-specific requirements for explanations.** Section 3.3 rigorously distinguishes interpretability from explainability (citing Rudin, 2019) and explicitly defines three requirements for deepfake audio detection—sample-specificity, time-specificity, and feature-specificity (identifying “unusual noise or errant formants”). This framework directly motivates the design choices in Sections 4 and 5 and provides a conceptual contribution beyond black-box performance metrics.

## Summary

The paper advances robust and explainable audio deepfake detection by adapting attention rollout to audio transformers and introducing a cross-dataset generalizability benchmark. Its single most important piece of evidence is Table 1, which demonstrates that when trained on ASVspoof 5 and evaluated on FakeAVCeleb, the AST transformer achieves a balanced F1 of 0.85 while the prevalent Wav2Vec architecture suffers from severe class skew and traditional GBDT methods fail near randomly—quantifying the real-world robustness gap and establishing that AST is surprisingly more robust out-of-distribution than prior state-of-the-art alternatives.

