### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

**1. Structural — The numerical training objective and sampling update are fundamentally inconsistent.**  
Equation (5) trains the denoising network $\boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}}$ to predict the noise $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}$, yet Algorithm 2 (line 12) computes the probability-flow ODE direction as $(\mathbf{x}^{\text{num}} - \boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}})/\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{\text{num}}$, which is the correct update only if $\boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}}$ predicts the clean data $\mathbf{x}_0$. For the variance-exploding ODE (Eq. 4), if $\boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}}$ predicts $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}$ then $d\mathbf{x}/d\sigma = \boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}}$; if it predicts $\mathbf{x}_0$ then $d\mathbf{x}/d\sigma = (\mathbf{x}-\boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}})/\sigma$. The paper cannot have it both ways. Because the sampler and the loss refer to two incompatible parameterizations, the core generative procedure as written is irreproducible. The strong empirical results suggest the implementation does not follow the text exactly, but a central methods paper cannot leave its training–sampling loop ambiguous. *(Eq. 5, Algorithm 2, Sec. 2.4)*

**2. Evidential — The evaluation is not self-contained, fair, or statistically validated.**  
With the exception of TabSyn (reproduced by the authors) and the Diabetes subset, all baseline numbers are imported directly from Zhang et al. (2024). This raises serious questions about fair comparison: differences in preprocessing, data splits, hyperparameter tuning, or random seeds can easily swamp the small absolute gaps reported (e.g., Shape on Default: 1.24 vs. 1.01; Trend on Magic: 0.76 vs. 0.88). The paper reports no statistical significance tests, and the headline “22.6% improvement” on Trend is a relative reduction of already-tiny error rates (2.33 → 1.80), making it highly sensitive to minor protocol variations. The claim of “superior average performance … across all eight metrics” is therefore not firmly established. *(Sec. 4.1, Tables 1–3, Table 1 footnote)*

**3. Evidential — The claim that learnable schedules adapt to feature heterogeneity is unsupported.**  
The paper’s central motivation for feature-wise schedules is that they “counteract the high heterogeneity across different feature distributions” (Sec. 2.3). The ablation (Table 5) shows only that learnable schedules outperform fixed ones, but this is confounded by the extra parameters introduced. The paper never analyzes the learned values of $\rho_i$ or $k_j$, nor shows any correlation between a feature’s marginal statistics (variance, skewness, category count) and its learned schedule. Without this, the schedules may simply act as per-feature capacity knobs rather than adaptive allocations that respect distributional disparity. *(Sec. 2.3, Table 5, Fig. 2)*

---

## Section-by-Section Notes

**Abstract & Introduction.** The paper repeatedly advertises a “joint continuous-time diffusion process” for mixed-type data. However, Eq. (1) shows the forward process factorizes independently across numerical and categorical features, $q(\mathbf{x}_t|\mathbf{x}_0)=q(\mathbf{x}_t^{\text{num}}|\mathbf{x}_0^{\text{num}})\cdot q(\mathbf{x}_t^{\text{cat}}|\mathbf{x}_0^{\text{cat}})$, and Eq. (2) shows the true reverse factorizes conditioned on $\mathbf{x}_0$. The only coupling is the shared neural network. This overstates the novelty relative to prior work (e.g., CoDi also uses separate forward processes with a shared backbone) and should be framed more precisely. *(Sec. 1, Eq. 1)*

**Section 2.2.** The categorical reverse posterior in Eq. (8) assumes that once a feature is unmasked it stays fixed. The stochastic sampler in Sec. 2.4 relaxes this, but the paper provides no theoretical argument that the resulting Markov chain preserves the correct marginal—especially since the reverse kernel in Eq. (8) is approximate. Citing continuous diffusion samplers (Karras et al., 2022) does not automatically justify the categorical case. *(Sec. 2.2, Sec. 2.4)*

**Section 2.3.** The relationship between the two categorical parameterizations—$\alpha_t=\exp(-\sigma^{\text{cat}}(t))$ in Sec. 2.2 and $\alpha_{k_j}^{\text{cat}}(t)=1-t^{k_j}$ in Eq. (11)—is never reconciled. The loss weights $\lambda_{\text{num}}$ and $\lambda_{\text{cat}}$ in Eq. (12) are introduced without any discussion of how they are set or whether they are tuned. *(Sec. 2.3, Eq. 11, Eq. 12)*

**Section 2.5.** The classifier-free guidance derivation in Eq. (13)–(16) assumes a single model $\theta$ that can be evaluated either conditionally or unconditionally. The actual implementation uses the full joint model (with $\mathbf{y}$ fixed) as the conditional model and a separate, smaller model trained only on the missing columns as the unconditional model. Because the two models have different architectures, inputs, and training distributions, the interpolation heuristic in Eq. (15)–(16) is not theoretically grounded, and the paper does not explain how the “small model” is trained. *(Sec. 2.5)*

**Section 3.** The characterization of TabSyn as suffering from “encoding overhead” and “low model capacity” is questionable given that TabSyn is the strongest baseline and outperforms TabDiff on Default and News Shape. The claim that no existing method explores “mixed-type diffusion framework in the continuous-time limit” is also too strong, since the forward process here is still separable across types. *(Sec. 3)*

**Section 4.1.** The evaluation metrics are deferred entirely to Appendix A.2. Because the main paper contains no definitions of Shape, Trend, $\alpha$-Precision, $\beta$-Recall, or Detection, it is not self-contained. The MLE protocol is acknowledged to be unreliable, yet it is still counted among the eight metrics used to claim overall superiority. *(Sec. 4.1)*

**Section 4.3.** Missing-value imputation is evaluated only on the single pre-defined target column of each dataset (the inherent classification/regression task). This is a highly restricted imputation setting that does not demonstrate the method’s ability to handle arbitrary missingness patterns, multiple simultaneous missing columns, or missing-not-at-random mechanisms. *(Sec. 4.3, Table 4)*

**Section 4.4.** The ablation averages results across all datasets, masking per-dataset variance. It also does not isolate whether the stochastic sampler helps because it adds useful noise or simply because it increases the effective number of function evaluations. *(Table 5)*

---

## Strengths

- **Principled mixed-type formulation.** The paper provides a clean, continuous-time diffusion formulation for both numerical and categorical features in their native representations, avoiding the latent-space encodings used by TabSyn. The use of an absorbing-state [MASK] process for categorical data is well-motivated and clearly described. *(Sec. 2.2)*
- **Feature-wise schedules as an inductive bias.** Regardless of whether the schedules truly “adapt,” introducing per-column noise parameters is a sensible way to give the model flexibility across heterogeneous tabular features, and the ablation shows consistent gains. *(Sec. 2.3, Table 5)*
- **Empirical breadth.** The experiments cover seven datasets and eight metrics, including fidelity, privacy, and downstream utility, which gives a holistic picture of generation quality. *(Sec. 4)*

---

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Correlation analysis for learned schedules.** Plot the learned $\rho_i$ and $k_j$ against feature statistics (standard deviation, skewness, category count). Without evidence that schedules correlate with distributional properties, the claim that they counteract heterogeneity is unverified. *(Sec. 2.3)*
2. **True missing-value imputation.** Evaluate on random missingness patterns (MCAR/MAR) with multiple missing columns per row, not just the single target column of the dataset’s built-in task. *(Sec. 4.3)*
3. **Self-contained baseline runs or significance tests.** Either re-run all baselines in the same codebase with identical splits and preprocessing, or report paired statistical significance for the small gaps against TabSyn. *(Sec. 4.1)*

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Controlled decomposition vs. TabSyn.** The paper needs to isolate whether gains come from native categorical modeling, the transformer architecture, the learnable schedules, or the stochastic sampler. A systematic swap-in/swap-out ablation against TabSyn’s architecture would clarify what actually drives performance. *(Sec. 4.4)*
2. **Convergence or bias analysis of the categorical stochastic sampler.** Show that the sampler preserves the correct stationary distribution, or at least characterize the bias introduced by the approximate reverse kernel. *(Sec. 2.4)*

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Learned schedule visualization.** For a representative dataset, plot $\sigma_i^{\text{num}}(t)$ and $\alpha_j^{\text{cat}}(t)$ across columns. This would immediately reveal whether the model uses the schedule capacity to adapt to different features. *(Sec. 2.3)*
2. **Failure-case analysis.** Examine the two datasets where TabDiff loses to TabSyn (Default and News Shape) to understand when native mixed-type modeling is disadvantaged. *(Sec. 4.2)*

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Resolve the training/sampling inconsistency.** Clarify whether $\boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}}$ predicts noise or clean data, and ensure Eq. (5) and Algorithm 2 agree. This is not optional for a methods paper. *(Sec. 2.2, Sec. 2.4)*
2. **Include a concise architecture specification in the main text.** The paper currently says only “parameterized by a transformer.” Readers need to know input dimensions, embedding strategy for one-hot vectors, number of layers, and attention details to assess reproducibility. *(Sec. 2.1)*

---

## Overall Assessment

The paper tackles an important problem and proposes several sensible ideas—native mixed-type diffusion, feature-wise schedules, and a stochastic categorical sampler. However, the submission is undermined by a critical inconsistency between the numerical training objective (Eq. 5) and the sampling algorithm (Algorithm 2), which renders the core method irreproducible as written. In addition, the experimental claims rely on externally sourced baseline numbers for most methods, and the central ablation does not disentangle capacity gains from true adaptive behavior of the learnable schedules. These issues are not minor clarifications; they strike at the reproducibility and validity of the contribution. I do not believe the paper should be accepted in its current form. If the authors can resolve the training–sampling mismatch, provide a fully self-contained and statistically validated evaluation, and demonstrate that the schedules meaningfully adapt to feature statistics, the work could become a strong contribution.
### Strength Finder
 ## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Feature-wise learnable noise schedules demonstrably improve fidelity.** Section 2.3 introduces per-column diffusion rates via power-mean schedules for numerical features (Eq. 10) and log-linear schedules for categorical features (Eq. 11). The ablation in Table 5 shows that enabling learnable schedules improves the average Shape error from 1.39 (fixed) to 1.24 (learnable) under deterministic sampling, while Figure 2 confirms these schedules achieve lower categorical and numerical training losses on Adult than fixed schedules.

2. **End-to-end continuous-time mixed-type diffusion in the original data space.** Unlike prior tabular diffusion models that rely on latent encoding (TabSyn) or discrete-time processes (TabDDPM, CoDi), the paper formulates a joint continuous-time forward process (Eq. 1–2) that couples a Variance Exploding SDE for numerical features (Eq. 3) with a masked absorbing process for categorical features (Eq. 6), trained via a single unified ELBO (Eq. 12). This avoids encoding overhead and yields a 22.6% average improvement in Trend (pair-wise correlation) over TabSyn (Table 2).

3. **A mixed-type stochastic sampler that reduces accumulated decoding error.** Section 2.4 and Algorithm 2 extend stochastic corrector ideas to categorical masked diffusion by interleaving an intermediate joint forward perturbation (steps 3–8) before each backward denoising step. The ablation in Table 5 isolates the sampler’s impact: with fixed schedules, switching from deterministic to stochastic sampling improves Shape error from 1.39 to 1.20 and Trend error from 2.29 to 1.93.

4. **Unified classifier-free guidance for mixed-type conditional generation.** Section 2.5 derives guidance rules adapted to both numerical (Eq. 15) and categorical (Eq. 16) features, enabling missing-value imputation without training a separate classifier. Table 4 validates that this CFG (\(\omega = 0.6\)) outperforms both unguided TABDIFF (\(\omega = 0.0\)) and TabSyn on five of seven datasets, delivering a 5.60% average improvement over the non-generative XGBoost baseline.

### Supporting strengths
1. **Consistent state-of-the-art performance across fidelity, downstream, and privacy metrics.** Across seven datasets and eight metrics, TABDIFF achieves the best average performance in every reported category—Shape (Table 1), Trend (Table 2), machine-learning efficiency (Table 3), and missing-value imputation (Table 4)—demonstrating that the native-space mixed-type framework generalizes broadly.

### Presentation / practical strengths (optional)
1. **Clear isolation of component contributions and visual evidence.** Table 5 jointly ablates the noise schedules and the stochastic sampler, while Figures 3 and 4 provide concrete visual evidence—density plots, histograms, and correlation heatmaps—that TABDIFF more accurately recovers both marginal distributions and pairwise column correlations than CoDi, TabDDPM, and TabSyn.

## Summary

TABDIFF introduces a continuous-time mixed-type diffusion model for tabular data that handles numerical and categorical features in their native formats through jointly trained, feature-wise learnable noise schedules and a mixed-type stochastic sampler. The single most compelling evidence for its effectiveness is the comprehensive sweep across seven datasets and eight metrics, where the model achieves state-of-the-art averages and delivers a 22.6% improvement in pair-wise column correlation (Trend metric, Table 2) over the prior best method, demonstrating that native-space continuous diffusion with adaptive feature-level schedules captures tabular heterogeneity better than latent-encoding or discrete-time alternatives.

--- Merged Review ---
## Summary

TABDIFF proposes a continuous-time diffusion model for mixed-type tabular data that operates in the original data space, avoiding latent encodings used by prior work. It combines a variance-exploding SDE for numerical features with a masked absorbing diffusion for categorical features, trained end-to-end via a unified ELBO. The paper introduces feature-wise learnable noise schedules and a mixed-type stochastic sampler, and reports state-of-the-art average performance across seven datasets and eight metrics.

## Strengths

- **Native-space continuous-time formulation for mixed-type data.** The paper avoids latent-space encodings by directly diffusion-modeling normalized numerical features and one-hot categorical features (with a [MASK] state) in their original representations, and trains both modalities with a single joint ELBO (Eq. 12). This is a clear practical advantage over latent-space methods such as TabSyn.
- **Feature-wise learnable schedules improve fidelity.** Table 5 shows that enabling learnable schedules reduces average Shape error from 1.39 (fixed, deterministic) to 1.24 (learnable, deterministic) and reduces training losses (Figure 2). This demonstrates that per-column schedule flexibility provides real empirical benefit.
- **Stochastic sampler improves generation quality.** Table 5 isolates the stochastic sampler’s impact: with fixed schedules, switching from deterministic to stochastic sampling improves Shape from 1.39 to 1.20 and Trend from 2.29 to 1.93. This is evidence that the proposed corrector-like mechanism helps.
- **Strong average empirical results.** Across seven datasets, TABDIFF achieves the best average Shape (1.17 vs. TabSyn 1.35) and Trend (1.80 vs. TabSyn 2.33), and competitive or best results on MLE and missing-value imputation (Tables 1–4).

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
None.

### Major
- **Critical inconsistency between numerical training objective and sampling algorithm.** Equation (5) trains the numerical denoising network $\boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}}$ to predict the noise $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}$, yet Algorithm 2 (line 12) computes the probability-flow direction as $(\mathbf{x}^{\text{num}} - \boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}})/\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{\text{num}}$, which is the correct ODE update only if $\boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}}$ predicts the clean data $\mathbf{x}_0$. For the variance-exploding formulation (Eq. 3–4), a noise-predicting model implies the ODE direction is $\boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}}$ itself (since $d\mathbf{x} = \boldsymbol{\epsilon}\, d\sigma$), whereas an $\mathbf{x}_0$-predicting model implies the direction $(\mathbf{x} - \boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}})/\sigma$. The paper cannot have it both ways as written. Because the sampler and the loss refer to two incompatible parameterizations, the core generative procedure is ambiguous and irreproducible from the text alone. This is a serious flaw for a methods paper.
- **Evaluation relies on externally sourced baselines without statistical validation.** With the exception of TabSyn (reproduced by the authors) and the Diabetes subset, all baseline numbers are imported directly from Zhang et al. (2024). This raises fair-comparison concerns: differences in preprocessing, data splits, or hyperparameter tuning can easily affect the small absolute gaps reported (e.g., Magic Trend: 0.88 vs. 0.76; Beijing Trend: 3.13 vs. 2.59). The paper reports no statistical significance tests, and the headline claim of “superior average performance … across all eight metrics” is therefore not firmly established.

### Minor
- **Central motivation for feature-wise schedules is not validated.** The paper claims the schedules “counteract the high heterogeneity across different feature distributions” (Sec. 2.3), but the ablation (Table 5) only shows that learnable schedules outperform fixed ones. It does not analyze the learned values of $\rho_i$ or $k_j$, nor show any correlation between a feature’s marginal statistics (variance, skewness, category count) and its learned schedule. Without this, the schedules may simply act as per-feature capacity knobs rather than adaptive allocations that respect distributional disparity.
- **Classifier-free guidance implementation deviates from its theoretical derivation.** Equations (13)–(16) derive CFG assuming a single model $\theta$ that can be evaluated either conditionally or unconditionally. The actual implementation uses the full joint model (with $\mathbf{y}$ fixed) as the conditional model and a separate, smaller model trained only on the missing columns as the unconditional model. Because the two models differ in architecture and training distribution, the interpolation in Eq. (15)–(16) is not theoretically grounded for this setup, and the paper does not discuss this approximation.
- **Categorical stochastic sampler lacks theoretical justification.** The approximate reverse kernel in Eq. (8) assumes that once a categorical feature is unmasked it stays fixed. The stochastic sampler in Sec. 2.4 relaxes this by re-perturbing decoded features, but the paper provides no argument that the resulting Markov chain preserves the correct marginal distribution. Citing continuous-diffusion samplers (Karras et al., 2022) does not automatically justify the categorical case.
- **MLE is acknowledged to be unreliable yet counted in the overall superiority claim.** The paper notes that “the MLE score evaluated under the current setting may not be a reliable indicator of data quality” (Sec. 4.3), yet MLE is still one of the eight metrics used to claim overall superiority. This weakens the strength of that aggregate claim.

### Trivial
- The loss weights $\lambda_{\text{num}}$ and $\lambda_{\text{cat}}$ in Eq. (12) are introduced without any discussion of how they are set or whether they are tuned.
- The relationship between the two categorical parameterizations—$\alpha_t=\exp(-\sigma^{\text{cat}}(t))$ in Sec. 2.2 and $\alpha_{k_j}^{\text{cat}}(t)=1-t^{k_j}$ in Eq. (11)—is left implicit rather than explicitly reconciled.
- The ablation in Table 5 averages results across all datasets, masking per-dataset variance.

## Nice-to-Have
- Correlation analysis plotting learned $\rho_i$ and $k_j$ against feature statistics (standard deviation, skewness, category count) to validate the heterogeneity-adaptation claim.
- Self-contained baseline re-runs or paired statistical significance tests for the small gaps against TabSyn.
- Architecture specification (input dimensions, embedding strategy, number of layers, attention details) in the main text to aid reproducibility.
- Evaluation on true missing-value imputation with random missingness patterns (MCAR/MAR) and multiple simultaneous missing columns, rather than only the single target column of each dataset’s built-in task.

## Removed Points
These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.

- **“Joint” forward process criticism.** The reviewer argues that Eq. (1) factorizes independently across numerical and categorical features, so calling it a “joint continuous-time diffusion process” overstates novelty. This misunderstands diffusion models: even standard image diffusion has a factorized forward process $q(\mathbf{x}_t|\mathbf{x}_0)=\prod_i q(x_t^i|x_0^i)$; the coupling arises in the learned reverse process. The paper’s usage is conventional and correct.
- **Claim that “no existing method explores mixed-type diffusion framework in the continuous-time limit” is too strong.** The reviewer says this is false because the forward process is separable. But the paper’s claim is about operating in the original data space (not latent) with continuous-time diffusion for mixed types. Prior work either uses latent encodings (TabSyn) or discrete-time processes (CoDi, TabDDPM). The claim is accurate in context.
- **Characterization of TabSyn as suffering from “encoding overhead” and “low model capacity.”** The reviewer finds this questionable because TabSyn wins on two datasets. However, the paper never singles out TabSyn for these criticisms; it describes latent-space methods generally, and TabDiff does outperform TabSyn on average. This criticism is a misreading.
- **Evaluation metrics deferred to Appendix A.2.** Under space constraints, deferring metric definitions to the appendix is standard practice and not a meaningful weakness.
- **Missing appendix / proofs.** The parser strips appendix sections; they exist in the original submission.

## Novel Insights

None beyond the paper's own contributions.

## Suggestions

1. **Clarify the training–sampling parameterization.** The authors must explicitly state whether $\boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}}$ predicts noise $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}$ or clean data $\mathbf{x}_0$, and ensure Eq. (5) and Algorithm 2 agree. If the network predicts $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}$, the sampler should use $d\mathbf{x}^{\text{num}} = \boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta^{\text{num}}\, d\sigma$ (or the equivalent discretization). If it predicts $\mathbf{x}_0$, Eq. (5) should be rewritten as a loss on $\mathbf{x}_0$ or the appropriate preconditioning should be shown.
2. **Add significance tests or self-contained baselines.** Either re-run all baselines with identical preprocessing and splits, or report confidence intervals and paired statistical tests (e.g., bootstrap or t-test on the 20 random samples) for the small average gaps, especially on Trend where several per-dataset margins are narrow.
3. **Validate the adaptive-schedule hypothesis.** Plot the learned schedule parameters against simple feature statistics. If they do not correlate, the paper should soften its claim from “counteract heterogeneity” to “increase per-feature capacity.”

## Score and Decision

**Calibration anchors:**
- **TabSyn** (`/home/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews/4Ay23yeuz0.md`, avg 6.75, Oral): Clean methodology, strong results, available code, well-written. TABDIFF has comparable empirical strength but is marred by the Eq. 5/Algorithm 2 inconsistency and weaker experimental controls, placing it clearly below TabSyn.
- **CDTD** (`/home/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews/QPtoBPn4lZ.md`, avg 5.50, Poster): Similar topic (continuous diffusion for mixed-type tabular data). Reviewers found it incremental but methodologically sound. TABDIFF introduces more novel components (native-space modeling, stochastic categorical sampler) and stronger average results, but pays a price in methodological clarity because of the training–sampling mismatch. Overall comparable to or slightly below CDTD.
- **TabDAR** (`/home/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews/kkGIbmpCHU.md`, avg 4.75, Reject): Strong empirical results but messy notation, correctness questions, and unclear methodology. TABDIFF is better written and its contributions are clearer, but it shares the liability of a concrete algorithmic inconsistency. TABDIFF sits above TabDAR.
- **CATDM** (`/home/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews/JD6j7XSluo.md`, avg 3.50, Withdrawn): Serious baseline discrepancies and weak overall performance. TABDIFF is well above this anchor.

**Reasoning:** TABDIFF presents genuinely useful ideas—native-space continuous diffusion, feature-wise schedules, and a stochastic categorical sampler—and backs them with strong average empirical results. However, the inconsistency between Eq. (5) and Algorithm 2 is a severe methodological flaw for a methods paper: a reader cannot implement the core algorithm from the text alone because the training objective and sampler assume incompatible network outputs. Combined with the reliance on externally sourced baseline numbers and the lack of statistical validation for small gaps, the paper falls below the standard of a clean accept. If the authors resolve the training–sampling mismatch and provide stronger experimental validation, the work would rise to the level of a strong poster or better.

